{"kind":"report","text":"Planner decision for 0784600e (what a refused late delivery writes), taking the checker's source corrections 91c866dc and b92bd90c.\n\nConfirmed, in these terms.\n\n1. \"Nothing written\" means no effect, send or attention of the handler. It does not remove the delivery's own record. A late delivery with no then is decided as every verified delivery is decided today (contract section 7.4; derive/delivery.ts decide): exactly one deciding entry with decision refused, no handler effects or sends, and exactly one authenticated result send to the sender, naming the request by its source fact and ordinal. The sender's refused clause receives that result with the reason. A duplicate of the delivery is answered from the deciding entry. Replay derives the same decision from the retained source entry's time and the entry's own time. With then present, the branch is applied instead of the handler and the entry carries late: { reason }. Never both for one delivery.\n\n2. The reason is { code: \"late\", name: \u003cthe declared reason\u003e }. \"late\" is a new value of RefusalReason (packages/contract/src/result.ts) beside guard-failed, because no guard was evaluated and a reader must tell the two apart without parsing the name. S1 already changes the contract for the form and the time operand; this is part of that change. spacing (P2) gets its own code the same way, and P2 names it.\n\n3. late judges a time. Under clock-behind the answer is unavailable with that reason before any entry, and the sender keeps the duty. That is the independent clock check of a time judgment, not the current refuse helper's judgesTime: false path, as the checker notes. Equal is not late and is judged by the handler alone.\n\n4. A signed act has no late. A refused act writes nothing, as today.\n\n5. Sequenced scopes (5.4 step 6, 5.9): the same distinction holds after allocation. A sequenced act refused at step 6 is as written: hole at P, refusal answered, pending row cleared, nothing in the member. A sequenced delivery refused at step 6: the scope writes the delivery's deciding entry (refused, no effects, its one result send) and clears the pending row in one transaction, then asks the order scope to record a hole at P with the reason. The deciding entry takes no position, since it is no effective fact of the group; it names P as the position it releases, so the hole request is idempotent and resumable: a deciding entry at the head that names a released position with no hole or expiry in the order journal asks for the hole again, as a prepare at the head asks for its resolution again. The entry is written in the room that fits was asked for at step 3 (it is smaller than the prepare it replaces), with no new reservation, and its result send is dispatched from it as a commit's sends are. The cost row \"refused after allocation\" becomes 2 in the order scope and 0 in the member for an act, 1 for a delivery. Witnesses: a refused sequenced delivery replays to the same deciding entry and result; a refused sequenced act to no entry.\n\n6. The two notes are corrected to say this (packages 4.1; sequenced 6.1, 5.4 step 6, 5.9) in a planner documentation head filed for checker review tonight together with the plan 033 slice-9 amendment. S1 does not wait for that landing: S1 implements points 1 to 4 now; S3 to S5 implement point 5."}